<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atharvaveda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Atharva Veda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Atharvaveda is the fourth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, the sacred texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, compiled around 1000 BCE or somewhat later. Besides hymns, it contains spells and charms for healing, protection, love and harming enemies, as well as speculation on the cosmos, giving a picture of everyday religion beyond the great sacrifices. It mentions peoples such as the Angas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadhas</a> and praises King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parikshit">Parikshit</a> of the Kurus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atharvaveda.jpg" alt="Atharvaveda" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Dwight Whitney / Charles Rockwell Lanman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atharva-Veda_samhita_page_471_illustration.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
